  "Question": "The fourth power of \\(\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt1}}\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\sqrt2+\\sqrt3\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\tfrac12(7+3\\sqrt5)\\)",
    "C": "\\(1+2\\sqrt3\\)",
    "D": "\\(3\\)",
    "E": "\\(3+2\\sqrt2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Start with the innermost radical: \\(\\sqrt1=1\\), so the next radical is \\(\\sqrt{1+1}=\\sqrt2\\). The given number is therefore \\(\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt2}\\). Its fourth power is the square of its square, so\n\\[\n\\left(\\sqrt{1+\\sqrt2}\\right)^4=(1+\\sqrt2)^2.\n\\]\nUsing \\((a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2\\), we obtain\n\\[\n(1+\\sqrt2)^2=1+2\\sqrt2+2=3+2\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{3+2\\sqrt2}\\), choice E.",
